1. Inspect CSV structure: Read offline_data/regional_sales.csv: list all column names, total row count, date range (min/max of the month column), and print first 5 rows as sample → db_agent
2. Compute 24-month baseline & anomaly multiples: Using the CSV, for each (region × product_line) compute the 24-month rolling mean of monthly sales, then compute the ratio = month_sales / 24-month_mean for every month. Save the full result table to a CSV file (e.g. offline_data/anomaly_ratios.csv) → code_agent
3. Identify top-3 anomalies & fetch return rates: From the anomaly_ratios.csv, find the top-3 (region × product_line × month) combinations with the highest ratio. For each, also extract the return_rate (or equivalent column) for that specific month from the original CSV. Save a small summary table to offline_data/top3_anomalies.csv → code_agent
4. Assess real demand vs channel stuffing: For each of the top-3 anomalies, compute supporting signals from the CSV: (a) return rate for the anomaly month vs the 24-month average return rate for that region×product_line, (b) whether the following 1-2 months show a sharp drop (reversal), (c) whether the spike is isolated or part of a trend. Save a structured assessment table to offline_data/stuffing_assessment.csv → code_agent
5. Write Markdown report with embedded data: Write a comprehensive Markdown report to offline_data/regional_sales_anomaly_report.md. Requirements: (1) H1 title; (2) at least one data table showing the top-3 anomalies with region, product_line, month, sales, 24-month mean, ratio, return_rate; (3) every key number must carry an inline source annotation in the format '（来源：offline_data/regional_sales.csv 的 <列名>，<聚合口径与样本行数>）' or '（来源：由前述两行计算）'; (4) a concluding paragraph judging whether each spike is more likely real demand or channel stuffing, with reasoning; (5) all conclusions derived solely from the local CSV. → code_agent
